 The `delete_token` is returned exactly once, in the `201` — no read ever discloses it.
 
+Two further optional fields protect a paste:
+
+- `burn_after_reading` — `true` or `false` only (anything else is `400`). A paste
+  marked `true` is **one-time**: its first successful read, in either form, returns it
+  exactly as an ordinary paste and then destroys it. Every later request for that
+  identifier — read, raw read, or revocation — is `404`. A read refused for a missing
+  or wrong password does *not* consume it. Absent means `false`.
+- `password` — text of 1 to 128 **characters** (the bound is characters, not encoded
+  bytes, so a 128-character password of emoji is accepted; anything else is `400`).
+  Reading the paste in either form then requires the `X-Paste-Password` header
+  holding exactly that value; a missing, empty, or wrong value is `403`. Revocation
+  still needs only the `X-Delete-Token` — the password never substitutes for it and
+  is not read on that route.
+
+```bash
+curl -s -X POST http://127.0.0.1:8080/pastes \
+     -d '{"content": "recovery code 4821", "burn_after_reading": true, "password": "hunter2"}'
+
+curl -s -H "X-Paste-Password: hunter2" http://127.0.0.1:8080/pastes/$ID/raw  # once only
+```
+
+The `201` body is unchanged — `{id, delete_token, url}` and nothing else. It never
+echoes the password nor reports either option. The structured read gains exactly two
+fields, `burn_after_reading` and `protected`, both present on every successful read
+including for pastes using neither option; `protected` reports only *whether* a
+password is required. The raw read is byte-for-byte unchanged, flags and all.
+
+An unknown, expired, revoked, or already-consumed identifier is `404` whatever
+password is supplied — never `403` — so a refusal never confirms that an identifier
+is real.
+
+There is **no way to change or recover a paste password.** Pastes are immutable; a
+forgotten password means the text is unreachable, and the only remaining operation is
+revocation with the delete token.
+
