 ## Storage and security notes
 
@@ -102,3 +146,23 @@ lock as the removal. An identifier from a request is matched against
 to lie inside the data directory. Expiry is computed per request against the current
 clock — there is no sweeper and no startup scan. Nothing the service returns contains
 a revocation secret, a filesystem path, or a stack trace.
+
+A paste password is never stored in recoverable form. The record holds only a
+verifier — `hashlib.pbkdf2_hmac("sha256", …)` at 600 000 iterations with a 16-byte
+salt drawn per paste from `secrets` and a 32-byte derived key — so two pastes created
+with the same password have unrelated stored hashes. Verification re-derives from the
+supplied text and compares the two digests with `hmac.compare_digest`, server-side, on
+every read of both forms; nothing is cached and no cookie or session is ever issued.
+No response, header, or error message contains a password, a salt, a stored hash, or
+the hashing parameters.
+
+A one-time paste is claimed with a single `os.unlink` under the same lock revocation
+holds: of N concurrent readers exactly one wins the directory entry, and the content
+is read into memory before the claim and written to the socket only after it, so a
+loser discards its copy without writing a byte. The password is checked *before* the
+claim, so a reader who cannot prove they hold it never consumes the single read. If
+the protection state of a stored record cannot be determined — a malformed flag or
+verifier, including an absurd iteration count — the paste reports `404` rather than
+being served unprotected or served twice. A record carrying neither key is a
+pre-feature record and reads back as neither one-time nor protected, with no
+migration.
